# Microsoft 365 license inventory and bulk license assignment from CSV

Two jobs in one script: first, an inventory of every subscribed SKU (purchased, consumed, free); second, an optional bulk assignment driven by a CSV with the columns UserPrincipalName and SkuPartNumber.

## What it does
- Reads subscribed SKUs with Get-MgSubscribedSku and writes an inventory CSV.
- If -InputCsv is given, validates each row, sets a missing usage location, checks that units are free and assigns the license with Set-MgUserLicense.
- Skips users who already hold the SKU and reports whether the license came directly or from a group.
- Honors -WhatIf, so you can preview the whole run; the inventory and the result log are still written during a -WhatIf run.

## Script
```powershell
<#
.SYNOPSIS
  License inventory and CSV-driven assignment.
.EXAMPLE
  .\Set-Licenses.ps1                                   # inventory only
  .\Set-Licenses.ps1 -InputCsv .\new-hires.csv -WhatIf # preview assignment
#>
[CmdletBinding(SupportsShouldProcess)]
param(
    [string]$InputCsv,
    [ValidatePattern('^[A-Z]{2}$')][string]$DefaultUsageLocation = 'DE',
    [string]$OutputFolder = '.'
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
# Inventory only needs a read scope; request the write scope only when a CSV is assigned
$scopes = @('Organization.Read.All')
if ($InputCsv) { $scopes += 'User.ReadWrite.All' }
try { Connect-MgGraph -Scopes $scopes -NoWelcome }
catch { Write-Error "Graph connection failed: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }

# --- Inventory ---
$skus = Get-MgSubscribedSku -All
$inventory = $skus | ForEach-Object {
    [pscustomobject]@{
        SkuPartNumber = $_.SkuPartNumber
        SkuId         = $_.SkuId
        Purchased     = $_.PrepaidUnits.Enabled
        Consumed      = $_.ConsumedUnits
        Available     = $_.PrepaidUnits.Enabled - $_.ConsumedUnits
        Suspended     = $_.PrepaidUnits.Suspended
        Warning       = $_.PrepaidUnits.Warning
    }
}
$invPath = Join-Path $OutputFolder "LicenseInventory_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd').csv"
$inventory | Sort-Object SkuPartNumber | Export-Csv $invPath -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8 -WhatIf:$false
$inventory | Sort-Object SkuPartNumber | Format-Table SkuPartNumber, Purchased, Consumed, Available -AutoSize

if (-not $InputCsv) { return }

# --- Assignment ---
$skuMap = @{}; foreach ($i in $inventory) { $skuMap[$i.SkuPartNumber] = $i }
$rows = Import-Csv $InputCsv
$log = foreach ($row in $rows) {
    $upn = $row.UserPrincipalName.Trim(); $part = $row.SkuPartNumber.Trim()
    $result = 'Assigned'
    try {
        if (-not $skuMap.ContainsKey($part)) { throw "Unknown SKU $part" }
        $sku  = $skuMap[$part]
        $user = Get-MgUser -UserId $upn -Property id,usageLocation,assignedLicenses,licenseAssignmentStates -ErrorAction Stop

        $existing = @($user.LicenseAssignmentStates | Where-Object { $_.SkuId -eq $sku.SkuId })
        if ($existing.Count -gt 0) {
            # a user can hold the same SKU directly and through a group at the same time
            $result = if (@($existing | Where-Object { -not $_.AssignedByGroup }).Count -gt 0) { 'AlreadyAssignedDirect' } else { 'AlreadyAssignedViaGroup' }
        }
        elseif ($sku.Available -le 0) { $result = 'NoLicensesAvailable' }
        else {
            if (-not $user.UsageLocation -and $PSCmdlet.ShouldProcess($upn, "Set usage location $DefaultUsageLocation")) {
                Update-MgUser -UserId $user.Id -UsageLocation $DefaultUsageLocation -ErrorAction Stop
            }
            if ($PSCmdlet.ShouldProcess($upn, "Assign $part")) {
                Set-MgUserLicense -UserId $user.Id -AddLicenses @(@{ SkuId = $sku.SkuId }) -RemoveLicenses @() -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null
                $sku.Available--
            }
            else { $result = 'WhatIf' }
        }
    }
    catch { $result = "Error: $($_.Exception.Message)" }
    [pscustomobject]@{ UserPrincipalName = $upn; Sku = $part; Result = $result }
}
$logPath = Join-Path $OutputFolder "LicenseAssignment_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd_HHmm').csv"
$log | Export-Csv $logPath -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8 -WhatIf:$false
$log | Group-Object Result | Select-Object Name, Count | Format-Table -AutoSize
```

## Required permissions/roles
- Delegated scopes: Organization.Read.All (SKUs) and User.ReadWrite.All (assignment).
- Role: License Administrator, User Administrator or Global Administrator.
- Inventory only needs Organization.Read.All and a reader role; the script requests User.ReadWrite.All only when -InputCsv is used. LicenseAssignment.ReadWrite.All is enough for assignment if every user already has a usage location.

## How to adapt
- Use group-based licensing for steady state and keep this script for exceptions, migrations and backfills.
- Remove licenses by passing the SkuId in -RemoveLicenses and an empty array in -AddLicenses.
- Disable individual service plans with @{ SkuId = ...; DisabledPlans = @('plan-guid') } in -AddLicenses.
- Extend the CSV with a DisabledPlans column if you assign trimmed-down suites.

## Common mistakes
- Missing usage location: assignment fails with a "invalid usage location" style error until it is set.
- Assigning directly to users who already receive the license via a group; later removal from the group will not remove the direct license and the cost stays.
- Using the display name of a product instead of SkuPartNumber (for example SPE_E3).
- Ignoring the Available count: assignments beyond purchased units fail or consume trial units unexpectedly.

# Find Microsoft Teams teams with no owner or a single owner

Ownerless teams cannot be managed: nobody approves members, renames channels or archives the team. This script finds them, and also catches the quieter risk of teams whose only owner has left or been disabled.

## What it does
- Lists all Microsoft 365 groups provisioned as Teams through the Graph resourceProvisioningOptions filter.
- Reads owners and member counts for each team.
- Looks up whether each owner account is still enabled.
- Assigns a risk label: NoOwner, OnlyDisabledOwners, SingleOwner or OK.
- Optionally adds a fallback owner (for example an IT governance mailbox user) to NoOwner teams, supporting -WhatIf.

## Script
```powershell
<#
.SYNOPSIS
  Reports Teams without a healthy owner set.
.EXAMPLE
  .\Get-TeamsOwnerHealth.ps1
  .\Get-TeamsOwnerHealth.ps1 -FallbackOwnerUpn 'teams-governance@contoso.com' -WhatIf
#>
[CmdletBinding(SupportsShouldProcess, ConfirmImpact = 'Medium')]
param(
    [string]$FallbackOwnerUpn,
    [string]$OutputPath = ".\TeamsOwnerHealth_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd').csv"
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
$scopes = @('Group.Read.All','User.Read.All')
if ($FallbackOwnerUpn) { $scopes = @('Group.ReadWrite.All','User.Read.All') }

try { Connect-MgGraph -Scopes $scopes -NoWelcome }
catch { Write-Error "Graph connection failed: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }

$fallback = $null
if ($FallbackOwnerUpn) {
    try { $fallback = Get-MgUser -UserId $FallbackOwnerUpn -Property id,userPrincipalName }
    catch { Write-Error "Fallback owner not found: $FallbackOwnerUpn"; return }
}

try {
    $teams = Get-MgGroup -Filter "resourceProvisioningOptions/Any(x:x eq 'Team')" -All `
             -Property id,displayName,visibility,createdDateTime,mail,renewedDateTime
}
catch { Write-Error "Could not list teams: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }
Write-Host "Checking $($teams.Count) teams..." -ForegroundColor Cyan

$userCache = @{}
$report = foreach ($t in $teams) {
    try {
        $owners = @(Get-MgGroupOwner -GroupId $t.Id -All -ErrorAction Stop)
        $memberCount = @(Get-MgGroupMember -GroupId $t.Id -All -ErrorAction Stop).Count
    }
    catch { Write-Warning "Skipping $($t.DisplayName): $($_.Exception.Message)"; continue }

    $enabled = 0; $names = @()
    foreach ($o in $owners) {
        if (-not $userCache.ContainsKey($o.Id)) {
            $userCache[$o.Id] = try { Get-MgUser -UserId $o.Id -Property id,accountEnabled,userPrincipalName -ErrorAction Stop } catch { $null }
        }
        $u = $userCache[$o.Id]
        if ($u) { $names += $u.UserPrincipalName; if ($u.AccountEnabled) { $enabled++ } }
    }

    $risk = if ($owners.Count -eq 0) { 'NoOwner' }
            elseif ($enabled -eq 0)  { 'OnlyDisabledOwners' }
            elseif ($enabled -eq 1)  { 'SingleOwner' }
            else { 'OK' }

    if ($fallback -and $risk -in 'NoOwner','OnlyDisabledOwners') {
        if ($PSCmdlet.ShouldProcess($t.DisplayName, "Add owner $FallbackOwnerUpn")) {
            try {
                New-MgGroupOwnerByRef -GroupId $t.Id -BodyParameter @{
                    '@odata.id' = "https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/users/$($fallback.Id)" } -ErrorAction Stop
                $risk += ' (fallback added)'
            }
            catch { Write-Warning "Owner add failed for $($t.DisplayName): $($_.Exception.Message)" }
        }
    }

    [pscustomobject]@{
        Team = $t.DisplayName; Visibility = $t.Visibility; Created = $t.CreatedDateTime
        OwnerCount = $owners.Count; EnabledOwners = $enabled; Owners = ($names -join ';')
        MemberCount = $memberCount; Risk = $risk; GroupId = $t.Id
    }
}
$report | Sort-Object Risk, Team | Export-Csv $OutputPath -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8 -WhatIf:$false
$report | Group-Object { ($_.Risk -split ' ')[0] } | Select-Object Name, Count | Format-Table -AutoSize
```

## Required permissions/roles
- Report: Group.Read.All and User.Read.All; roles Global Reader or Teams Administrator.
- Fixing owners: Group.ReadWrite.All; roles Groups Administrator, Teams Administrator or Global Administrator.

## How to adapt
- Instead of a fixed fallback owner, promote the longest-standing member by reading group members and calling New-MgGroupOwnerByRef for that user.
- Combine with Get-MgGroup -Property renewedDateTime and expiration policy data to spot teams that will expire soon.
- Send each team's remaining owner a mail via Send-MgUserMail reminding them to add a second owner.
- Add a column for the sensitivity label using the assignedLabels property.

## Common mistakes
- Using Get-MgTeam for the list: it returns fewer properties and does not expose owners the same way. Query groups instead.
- Counting guests as owners: guests cannot be Team owners in some tenant configurations, so check owner userType.
- Adding a mailbox or service account as owner without a license or sign-in plan, so it cannot approve anything.
- Forgetting private channels and shared channels have their own owners, which this script does not inspect.

# SharePoint Online permission audit with PnP PowerShell

This script inventories who has access to a site and to every list or library that breaks permission inheritance, and flags the principals that usually signal oversharing. It is read-only.

## What it does
- Connects to each site you pass with interactive sign-in and your own Entra app registration.
- Reads site-level role assignments and expands SharePoint groups into their members.
- Finds lists and libraries with unique permissions and reports their role assignments as well.
- Flags Everyone, Everyone except external users, and external (#ext#) accounts.
- Writes one flat CSV: Site, Scope, Object, Principal, PrincipalType, PermissionLevels, Flag.

## Script
```powershell
<#
.SYNOPSIS
  Read-only permission audit for SharePoint Online sites.
.EXAMPLE
  .\Get-SpPermissionAudit.ps1 -SiteUrl 'https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/hr' -ClientId '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000'
#>
[CmdletBinding()]
param(
    [Parameter(Mandatory)][string[]]$SiteUrl,
    [Parameter(Mandatory)][string]$ClientId,      # your PnP Entra app registration
    [switch]$SkipLists,
    [string]$OutputPath = ".\SpPermissionAudit_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd').csv"
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'

function Get-Flag([string]$login, [string]$type) {
    if ($login -match 'spo-grid-all-users')       { return 'EveryoneExceptExternal' }
    if ($login -match '^c:0\(\.s\|true$')           { return 'Everyone' }
    if ($login -match '#ext#')                      { return 'ExternalUser' }
    return ''
}

function Get-AssignmentRows($clientObject, [string]$site, [string]$scope, [string]$objectName) {
    $assignments = Get-PnPProperty -ClientObject $clientObject -Property RoleAssignments
    foreach ($ra in $assignments) {
        Get-PnPProperty -ClientObject $ra -Property RoleDefinitionBindings, Member | Out-Null
        $levels = ($ra.RoleDefinitionBindings | ForEach-Object { $_.Name }) -join ';'
        $m = $ra.Member
        if ($m.PrincipalType -eq 'SharePointGroup') {
            $members = @()
            try { $members = @(Get-PnPGroupMember -Group $m.Title -ErrorAction Stop) } catch { }
            if ($members.Count -eq 0) {
                [pscustomobject]@{ Site=$site; Scope=$scope; Object=$objectName; Principal="$($m.Title) (empty group)"
                    PrincipalType='SharePointGroup'; PermissionLevels=$levels; Flag='' }
            }
            foreach ($u in $members) {
                [pscustomobject]@{ Site=$site; Scope=$scope; Object=$objectName; Principal="$($u.Title) via $($m.Title)"
                    PrincipalType=$u.PrincipalType; PermissionLevels=$levels; Flag=(Get-Flag $u.LoginName $u.PrincipalType) }
            }
        }
        else {
            [pscustomobject]@{ Site=$site; Scope=$scope; Object=$objectName; Principal=$m.Title
                PrincipalType=$m.PrincipalType; PermissionLevels=$levels; Flag=(Get-Flag $m.LoginName $m.PrincipalType) }
        }
    }
}

$all = foreach ($url in $SiteUrl) {
    try {
        Connect-PnPOnline -Url $url -Interactive -ClientId $ClientId
        $web = Get-PnPWeb
        Get-AssignmentRows -clientObject $web -site $url -scope 'Site' -objectName $web.Title

        if (-not $SkipLists) {
            $lists = Get-PnPList -Includes HasUniqueRoleAssignments | Where-Object { -not $_.Hidden -and $_.HasUniqueRoleAssignments }
            foreach ($l in $lists) {
                Get-AssignmentRows -clientObject $l -site $url -scope 'List' -objectName $l.Title
            }
        }
    }
    catch { Write-Warning "Failed for ${url}: $($_.Exception.Message)" }
}

$all | Export-Csv $OutputPath -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8
Write-Host "Rows: $(@($all).Count). Flagged: $(@($all | Where-Object Flag).Count). Output: $OutputPath" -ForegroundColor Green
$all | Where-Object Flag | Format-Table Site, Object, Principal, Flag, PermissionLevels -AutoSize
```

## Required permissions/roles
- Run as a Site Collection Administrator (or SharePoint Administrator) of every audited site; reading role assignments needs Manage Permissions style access.
- PnP.PowerShell needs an Entra app registration (-ClientId) with delegated SharePoint permissions and consent; since 2024 PnP no longer ships a shared multi-tenant app.
- For tenant-wide runs, enumerate sites first with Connect-PnPOnline to the admin URL and Get-PnPTenantSite.

## How to adapt
- Add item-level checks for sensitive libraries with Get-PnPListItem and HasUniqueRoleAssignments, but expect long runtimes.
- Pair with sharing link reports (Get-PnPFileSharingLink or the SharePoint Advanced Management data access governance reports) for Anyone and company-wide links.
- Replace the flag logic with a list of your own sensitive security groups to find "broad group on confidential site" cases.
- Schedule it against Hub site members and diff the CSV against last month.

## Common mistakes
- Auditing only site-level access: most leaks come from broken inheritance on libraries and folders.
- Treating empty SharePoint groups as harmless; they are often the leftover of an unfinished cleanup.
- Forgetting that Microsoft 365 group members are not listed as SharePoint group members, so the Members group shows a single "group" principal that must be expanded in Entra.
- Running without an own -ClientId and wondering why sign-in fails.

# Azure tag compliance report across subscriptions with optional remediation

Tags drive cost allocation, ownership and automation. This script reports which resources and resource groups lack the tags your governance standard requires, and can optionally merge default values.

## What it does
- Iterates over the subscriptions you name (or all enabled subscriptions you can see).
- Checks every resource, and optionally resource groups, for required tag names (case-insensitive, like Azure) and empty values.
- Exports a CSV with the missing tags per resource.
- With -ApplyDefaults it merges default values for missing tags using Update-AzTag, honoring -WhatIf.

## Script
```powershell
<#
.SYNOPSIS
  Tag compliance report for Azure resources.
.EXAMPLE
  .\Get-AzTagCompliance.ps1 -RequiredTags CostCenter,Owner,Environment -IncludeResourceGroups
  .\Get-AzTagCompliance.ps1 -RequiredTags Environment -DefaultTagValues @{Environment='unassigned'} -ApplyDefaults -WhatIf
#>
[CmdletBinding(SupportsShouldProcess, ConfirmImpact = 'Medium')]
param(
    [Parameter(Mandatory)][string[]]$RequiredTags,
    [string[]]$SubscriptionId,
    [hashtable]$DefaultTagValues = @{},
    [switch]$IncludeResourceGroups,
    [switch]$ApplyDefaults,
    [string]$OutputPath = ".\AzTagCompliance_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd').csv"
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'

# Sign in only if there is no active context (no secrets in the script)
if (-not (Get-AzContext)) {
    # sign-in only reads; run it with WhatIf off so a -WhatIf run does not skip it
    try { & { $WhatIfPreference = $false; Connect-AzAccount -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null } }
    catch { Write-Error "Azure sign-in failed: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }
}

try {
    $subs = if ($SubscriptionId) { $SubscriptionId | ForEach-Object { Get-AzSubscription -SubscriptionId $_ } }
            else { Get-AzSubscription | Where-Object State -eq 'Enabled' }
}
catch { Write-Error "Could not list subscriptions: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }

function Get-MissingTags($tags, [string[]]$required) {
    foreach ($name in $required) {
        $key = if ($tags) { $tags.Keys | Where-Object { $_ -ieq $name } | Select-Object -First 1 }
        if (-not $key -or [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace([string]$tags[$key])) { $name }
    }
}

$report = foreach ($sub in $subs) {
    Write-Host "Subscription: $($sub.Name)" -ForegroundColor Cyan
    # Set-AzContext honors -WhatIf; without this, a -WhatIf run would keep scanning the first subscription
    try { & { $WhatIfPreference = $false; Set-AzContext -SubscriptionId $sub.Id -TenantId $sub.TenantId -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null } }
    catch { Write-Warning "Skipping $($sub.Name): $($_.Exception.Message)"; continue }

    try { $resources = @(Get-AzResource -ErrorAction Stop) }
    catch { Write-Warning "Could not list resources in $($sub.Name): $($_.Exception.Message)"; continue }
    $targets = @($resources |
        ForEach-Object { [pscustomobject]@{ Type=$_.ResourceType; Name=$_.Name; Rg=$_.ResourceGroupName; Id=$_.ResourceId; Tags=$_.Tags } })
    if ($IncludeResourceGroups) {
        $targets += @(Get-AzResourceGroup | ForEach-Object {
            [pscustomobject]@{ Type='resourceGroup'; Name=$_.ResourceGroupName; Rg=$_.ResourceGroupName; Id=$_.ResourceId; Tags=$_.Tags } })
    }

    foreach ($t in $targets) {
        $missing = @(Get-MissingTags $t.Tags $RequiredTags)
        if ($missing.Count -eq 0) { continue }
        $action = ''
        if ($ApplyDefaults) {
            $merge = @{}
            foreach ($m in $missing) { if ($DefaultTagValues.ContainsKey($m)) { $merge[$m] = $DefaultTagValues[$m] } }
            if ($merge.Count -gt 0 -and $PSCmdlet.ShouldProcess($t.Id, "Merge tags: $($merge.Keys -join ',')")) {
                try { Update-AzTag -ResourceId $t.Id -Tag $merge -Operation Merge -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null; $action = 'DefaultsApplied' }
                catch { $action = "Failed: $($_.Exception.Message)" }
            }
        }
        [pscustomobject]@{
            Subscription = $sub.Name; ResourceGroup = $t.Rg; Type = $t.Type; Name = $t.Name
            MissingTags = ($missing -join ';'); Action = $action; ResourceId = $t.Id
        }
    }
}
$report | Export-Csv $OutputPath -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8 -WhatIf:$false
Write-Host "Non-compliant objects: $(@($report).Count). Output: $OutputPath" -ForegroundColor Green
$report | Group-Object Subscription | Select-Object Name, Count | Format-Table -AutoSize
```

## Required permissions/roles
- Reader on the subscriptions for the report.
- Tag Contributor (or Contributor) on the scope for -ApplyDefaults.
- Modules: Az.Accounts and Az.Resources.

## How to adapt
- For very large estates use Azure Resource Graph (Search-AzGraph from Az.ResourceGraph) instead of Get-AzResource; it is much faster.
- Enforce the standard permanently with Azure Policy (for example "Require a tag on resources" and "Inherit a tag from the resource group" with modify effect).
- Look up owners from a CMDB CSV and pass per-resource-group values instead of one static default.
- Run it in Azure Automation with a managed identity that has Reader.

## Common mistakes
- Treating tag names as case-sensitive: Azure tag names are case-insensitive, values are case-sensitive.
- Expecting resource group tags to flow to resources automatically; they do not without policy.
- Applying meaningless defaults such as "unknown" everywhere, which hides the problem instead of fixing it.
- Forgetting that some resource types do not support tags, so they appear as permanent exceptions.
- Expecting -WhatIf to switch everything off: cmdlets such as Set-AzContext, Connect-AzAccount and Export-Csv inherit it and would be skipped. The script suppresses WhatIf for these read-only steps and applies it only to the tag changes.

# Exchange Online mailbox forwarding and inbox rule audit

Attackers who compromise a mailbox often add a hidden inbox rule that forwards mail outside. Users also forward to private accounts for convenience. This script checks all three places where forwarding hides.

## What it does
- Reads ForwardingSmtpAddress, ForwardingAddress and DeliverToMailboxAndForward from all mailboxes and resolves ForwardingAddress recipients (mailboxes or mail contacts) to their primary SMTP address.
- Reads inbox rules (including hidden ones) with ForwardTo, ForwardAsAttachmentTo or RedirectTo.
- Lists transport rules that redirect or blind-copy mail.
- Extracts SMTP addresses and marks them External when the domain is not one of your accepted domains.
- Shows the AutoForwardingMode of the outbound spam policies, which decides whether external forwarding is actually blocked.

## Script
```powershell
<#
.SYNOPSIS
  Audits mailbox forwarding, inbox rules and transport rules.
.EXAMPLE
  .\Get-ForwardingAudit.ps1 -OutputFolder C:\Temp
  .\Get-ForwardingAudit.ps1 -SkipInboxRules          # fast mode, settings only
#>
[CmdletBinding()]
param(
    [switch]$SkipInboxRules,
    [string]$OutputFolder = '.'
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
try { Connect-ExchangeOnline -ShowBanner:$false }
catch { Write-Error "Exchange Online connection failed: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }

$accepted = @(Get-AcceptedDomain | ForEach-Object { $_.DomainName.ToString().ToLower() })
$mailRegex = '[A-Za-z0-9._%+\-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.\-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}'

function Get-Addresses($values) {
    if (-not $values) { return @() }
    [regex]::Matches((@($values) -join ' '), $mailRegex) | ForEach-Object { $_.Value.ToLower() } | Select-Object -Unique
}
function Test-External([string[]]$addresses) {
    foreach ($a in $addresses) { if ($accepted -notcontains ($a -split '@')[-1]) { return $true } }
    return $false
}

$stamp = Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd'
$findings = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]

# 1. Mailbox-level forwarding
$mailboxes = Get-EXOMailbox -ResultSize Unlimited -RecipientTypeDetails UserMailbox, SharedMailbox `
    -Properties ForwardingAddress, ForwardingSmtpAddress, DeliverToMailboxAndForward
foreach ($m in $mailboxes) {
    if ($m.ForwardingSmtpAddress -or $m.ForwardingAddress) {
        $targets = @($m.ForwardingSmtpAddress, $m.ForwardingAddress) | Where-Object { $_ }
        $addr = @(Get-Addresses $targets)
        if ($m.ForwardingAddress) {
            # ForwardingAddress holds a recipient (mailbox or mail contact), not an SMTP string: resolve its primary address
            try { $addr += (Get-Recipient -Identity $m.ForwardingAddress -ErrorAction Stop).PrimarySmtpAddress.ToString().ToLower() }
            catch { Write-Warning "Could not resolve ForwardingAddress of $($m.UserPrincipalName): $($_.Exception.Message)" }
        }
        $findings.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Mailbox=$m.UserPrincipalName; Source='MailboxForwarding'; Name='';
            Target=($targets -join ';'); KeepsCopy=$m.DeliverToMailboxAndForward
            External=[bool](Test-External $addr); Enabled=$true })
    }
}

# 2. Inbox rules (slow on large tenants)
if (-not $SkipInboxRules) {
    $i = 0
    foreach ($m in $mailboxes) {
        $i++; if ($i % 100 -eq 0) { Write-Progress -Activity 'Inbox rules' -Status "$i of $($mailboxes.Count)" -PercentComplete (100 * $i / $mailboxes.Count) }
        try { $rules = Get-InboxRule -Mailbox $m.UserPrincipalName -IncludeHidden -ErrorAction Stop }
        catch { Write-Warning "Rules failed for $($m.UserPrincipalName): $($_.Exception.Message)"; continue }
        foreach ($r in $rules | Where-Object { $_.ForwardTo -or $_.ForwardAsAttachmentTo -or $_.RedirectTo }) {
            $targets = @($r.ForwardTo) + @($r.ForwardAsAttachmentTo) + @($r.RedirectTo) | Where-Object { $_ }
            $findings.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Mailbox=$m.UserPrincipalName; Source='InboxRule'; Name=$r.Name
                Target=($targets -join ';'); KeepsCopy=''; External=[bool](Test-External (Get-Addresses $targets)); Enabled=$r.Enabled })
        }
    }
}

# 3. Transport rules
foreach ($t in Get-TransportRule | Where-Object { $_.RedirectMessageTo -or $_.BlindCopyTo -or $_.CopyTo }) {
    $targets = @($t.RedirectMessageTo) + @($t.BlindCopyTo) + @($t.CopyTo) | Where-Object { $_ }
    $findings.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Mailbox='(organization)'; Source='TransportRule'; Name=$t.Name
        Target=($targets -join ';'); KeepsCopy=''; External=[bool](Test-External (Get-Addresses $targets)); Enabled=($t.State -eq 'Enabled') })
}

$path = Join-Path $OutputFolder "ForwardingAudit_$stamp.csv"
$findings | Sort-Object External -Descending | Export-Csv $path -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8
Write-Host "Findings: $($findings.Count), external: $(@($findings | Where-Object External).Count). Output: $path" -ForegroundColor Green

Get-HostedOutboundSpamFilterPolicy | Select-Object Name, AutoForwardingMode | Format-Table -AutoSize
Disconnect-ExchangeOnline -Confirm:$false
```

## Required permissions/roles
- Exchange Online roles: a read-only role group such as View-Only Organization Management, or Global Reader, covers the recipient and configuration cmdlets used here. Check Get-InboxRule and Get-TransportRule in the Exchange permissions reference for your role group; if one is denied, add the role it names.
- Module: ExchangeOnlineManagement 3.x. Sign-in is interactive with modern authentication.

## How to adapt
- Remediate with Set-Mailbox -Identity -ForwardingSmtpAddress $null -ForwardingAddress $null and Remove-InboxRule, wrapped in ShouldProcess and a ticket reference.
- Block external auto-forwarding tenant-wide with Set-HostedOutboundSpamFilterPolicy -AutoForwardingMode Off after you review legitimate cases.
- Add Search-UnifiedAuditLog for New-InboxRule and Set-InboxRule events to find who created suspicious rules and when.
- Run weekly and alert when a new External finding appears.

## Common mistakes
- Checking only mailbox forwarding: most malicious forwarding is done through inbox rules.
- Forgetting -IncludeHidden, which is where attackers hide rules from the Outlook UI.
- Assuming forwarding to an internal contact object is safe; check whether that contact points to an external address.
- Disabling forwarding without telling the user first, which breaks legitimate business workflows.

# Entra app registration secret and certificate expiry report

Expired client secrets cause silent outages: a flow stops, an integration fails, a deployment pipeline breaks. This script finds credentials that have expired or will expire soon, and names the owners to contact.

## What it does
- Reads all application registrations with their password credentials (client secrets) and key credentials (certificates).
- Calculates days until expiry and classifies each credential as Expired, ExpiringSoon or OK.
- Resolves application owners so the report can be routed to a person.
- Optionally includes service principals, which covers SAML signing certificates of enterprise applications.
- Writes a CSV sorted by urgency.

## Script
```powershell
<#
.SYNOPSIS
  Reports expiring and expired app registration credentials.
.EXAMPLE
  .\Get-AppCredentialExpiry.ps1 -WarnDays 45 -IncludeEnterpriseApps
#>
[CmdletBinding()]
param(
    [ValidateRange(1,730)][int]$WarnDays = 30,
    [switch]$IncludeEnterpriseApps,
    [switch]$ExpiringOnly,
    [string]$OutputPath = ".\AppCredentialExpiry_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd').csv"
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
try { Connect-MgGraph -Scopes 'Application.Read.All' -NoWelcome }
catch { Write-Error "Graph connection failed: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }

$now = Get-Date
function Get-Status([datetime]$end) {
    if ($end -lt $now) { 'Expired' }
    elseif ($end -lt $now.AddDays($WarnDays)) { 'ExpiringSoon' }
    else { 'OK' }
}

$ownerCache = @{}
function Get-OwnerList($objectId, [string]$kind) {
    if ($ownerCache.ContainsKey($objectId)) { return $ownerCache[$objectId] }
    try {
        $owners = if ($kind -eq 'App') { Get-MgApplicationOwner -ApplicationId $objectId -All -ErrorAction Stop }
                  else { Get-MgServicePrincipalOwner -ServicePrincipalId $objectId -All -ErrorAction Stop }
        $text = ($owners | ForEach-Object { $_.AdditionalProperties['userPrincipalName'] } | Where-Object { $_ }) -join ';'
    }
    catch { $text = '(owner lookup failed)' }
    $ownerCache[$objectId] = $text   # one lookup per object, not per credential
    return $text
}

function New-Row($obj, [string]$kind, $cred, [string]$credType) {
    [pscustomobject]@{
        ObjectType    = $kind
        DisplayName   = $obj.DisplayName
        AppId         = $obj.AppId
        CredentialType= $credType
        CredentialName= $cred.DisplayName
        KeyId         = $cred.KeyId
        Start         = $cred.StartDateTime
        End           = $cred.EndDateTime
        DaysLeft      = [int]($cred.EndDateTime - $now).TotalDays
        Status        = Get-Status $cred.EndDateTime
        Owners        = Get-OwnerList $obj.Id $kind
        ObjectId      = $obj.Id
    }
}

try {
    $apps = Get-MgApplication -All -PageSize 500 -Property id,appId,displayName,passwordCredentials,keyCredentials
}
catch { Write-Error "Could not read applications: $($_.Exception.Message)"; return }

$rows = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
foreach ($a in $apps) {
    foreach ($c in $a.PasswordCredentials) { $rows.Add((New-Row $a 'App' $c 'Secret')) }
    foreach ($c in $a.KeyCredentials)      { $rows.Add((New-Row $a 'App' $c 'Certificate')) }
}

if ($IncludeEnterpriseApps) {
    $sps = Get-MgServicePrincipal -All -PageSize 500 -Property id,appId,displayName,passwordCredentials,keyCredentials
    foreach ($s in $sps) {
        foreach ($c in $s.PasswordCredentials) { $rows.Add((New-Row $s 'ServicePrincipal' $c 'Secret')) }
        foreach ($c in $s.KeyCredentials)      { $rows.Add((New-Row $s 'ServicePrincipal' $c 'Certificate')) }
    }
}

$result = $rows
if ($ExpiringOnly) { $result = $rows | Where-Object { $_.Status -ne 'OK' } }
$result | Sort-Object DaysLeft | Export-Csv $OutputPath -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8

Write-Host "Credentials checked: $($rows.Count). Output: $OutputPath" -ForegroundColor Green
$rows | Group-Object Status | Select-Object Name, Count | Format-Table -AutoSize
$rows | Where-Object { $_.Status -ne 'OK' } | Sort-Object DaysLeft |
    Select-Object -First 20 DisplayName, CredentialType, End, DaysLeft, Owners | Format-Table -AutoSize
```

## Required permissions/roles
- Delegated scope Application.Read.All with admin consent.
- Roles: Application Administrator, Cloud Application Administrator, Global Reader or Security Reader.

## How to adapt
- Automate the alert: run it in Azure Automation with a managed identity (Application.Read.All application permission) and send the ExpiringSoon rows to Teams or mail.
- Rotate with Add-MgApplicationPassword and Remove-MgApplicationPassword, storing the new secret in Key Vault and never in the script.
- Prefer certificates or federated credentials (workload identity federation) over secrets to remove expiry from the picture.
- Filter by owner to send each person only their own applications.

## Common mistakes
- Reading Get-MgApplicationOwner output as full user objects: UPN sits in AdditionalProperties.
- Using the AppId where Graph expects the object Id (-ApplicationId takes the object Id).
- Ignoring apps without owners. Those are the ones nobody will renew, so assign a team owner.
- Removing the old secret before the consuming system has switched to the new one.
